notes = [
{"title": "The STAR Method: Structure Every Behavioral Answer",
"body": "Behavioral questions are scored on structure, not just content. STAR: Situation (one sentence of context), Task (what you were asked to do), Action (what YOU did — the longest part, first person, specific verbs), Result (measurable outcome + what you learned). Timebox: 90 seconds to 2 minutes; longer rambles lose the panel. The most common failure is staying in Situation for a minute and compressing Action into ten seconds — invert that ratio. Worked example, full model answer for 'Tell me about a time you translated unclear business requirements into technical deliverables.' Situation: 'Our ops team asked for \"better visibility into deployments\" with no further detail.' Task: 'I needed to turn that into something shippable.' Action: 'I interviewed three on-call engineers for 20 minutes each, mapped their pain to the four golden signals, and proposed a one-page spec: deploy annotations on every dashboard plus a burn-rate alert per service. I validated the spec with the requester before writing code, then built it in two sprints.' Result: 'Deploy-related incidents dropped from \"we didn't know what shipped\" to a 5-minute correlation, and the one-page spec format became the team's template for vague requests.' Notice: specific verbs (interviewed, mapped, proposed, validated, built), a number, and a transferable lesson. Practice every behavioral answer in this shape until it is muscle memory — the drills below score you on it."},
{"title": "STAR Scaffolds: The Three Behavioral Questions",
"body": "Three behavioral questions appear in the panel list; here are complete STAR scaffolds you adapt to your real experience — never invent employers or events, but do bring real ones. (1) 'Translate unclear business requirements into technical deliverables': use the scaffold above — interview stakeholders, write the one-page spec, validate before building, ship incrementally, measure. Your Action must show the translation mechanism (questions asked, spec written), not just 'I figured it out.' (2) 'Introduce a new technology or methodology that improved team performance': Situation — team hand-editing YAML per environment, slow and error-prone. Task — evaluate and introduce a safer way. Action — 'I built a proof-of-concept of Kustomize overlays on one service, demoed the diff to the team, wrote the migration guide, and paired with each service owner for their first conversion.' Result — 'Deploy misconfigurations fell, onboarding a new service went from a day to an hour, and the pattern spread to all 12 services.' The key verb is 'paired' — adoption, not just invention. (3) 'Mentor or upskill developers': Situation — junior engineers struggling with the on-call rotation. Task — get them confident. Action — 'I ran a weekly 30-minute session: week 1 reading dashboards, week 2 the Pending-pod runbook hands-on, week 3 a simulated incident they commanded while I observed.' Result — 'Two juniors took solo on-call within two months with zero escalations in their first rotations.' Mentoring answers must show a curriculum and an outcome, not 'I helped people.'"},
{"title": "'Do You Prefer Coding or Infrastructure?' — The Honest Answer",
"body": "This behavioral question is a trap only if you treat it as one. The panel is checking self-awareness and role fit, not loyalty to a tribe. The strong answer is honest, specific, and tied to the role: 'I like both, but I am strongest and happiest at the boundary — writing code that operates infrastructure. I write Python and Go tooling, but I reach for it to solve operational problems: automation, controllers, pipeline logic. Pure application feature work is fine, but my best days are when code I wrote at 10 AM prevents a 2 AM page.' Then bridge to evidence: 'That is why my recent work is Terraform modules, a Prometheus exporter, and the incident runbooks — code in service of reliability.' What not to say: 'I hate coding' (for a DevSecOps role that is disqualifying), 'I only want to code' (wrong role), or a hedged non-answer ('both are great!'). If you genuinely prefer one side, say so and explain why the role still fits: 'I prefer infrastructure, and I code to automate it — which is exactly what this team's platform work needs.' The mock task below drills this as the panel would ask it, with the STAR-shaped reasoning in the explanation."},
{"title": "Delivery Coaching: Sound Like You Know It Cold",
"body": "Content without delivery fails panels. Four rules, practiced aloud, recorded, reviewed. (1) Structure first: open every technical answer with the headline, then the points — 'Three reasons: X, Y, Z' — then stop. Panels score organized answers; rambling signals uncertainty even when the facts are right. (2) Slow down: nerves accelerate speech; deliberately speak at two-thirds speed, pause between points, and let the panel absorb. A two-second pause reads as confidence, not hesitation. (3) Camera and presence: look at the camera, not your notes; sit upright; answer as if you are explaining to a colleague, not reciting. Never read — if you must glance at notes, pause, glance, then re-engage the camera and speak. (4) Cold-answer protocol: no copilot, no docs, no pausing the timer. In the drills below, set a timer (60-90 seconds per item), answer aloud, then check the model answer. Mark every item red/yellow/green: red means you could not start, yellow means partial, green means fluent. Reds become tomorrow's study list; the goal is zero reds before you interview. Record yourself weekly — the cringe is the curriculum."},
{"title": "The 90-Minute Mock Panel Script (Part 1: Technical Rounds)",
"body": "Run this solo, timed, aloud, no aids — it is the full panel compressed. Round 1 — Introduction (0:00-0:10): deliver your 60-second 'tell me about yourself' (present role, two proof points, why this role), then 2 minutes on your proudest infrastructure project, STAR-shaped. Round 2 — Terraform/IaC deep (0:10-0:30): Terraform vs Ansible and why not just Ansible; removing resources (destroy vs remove block + apply); the storage-account scenario with per-container access types (for_each list to map); state drift and the import with for_each address escaping; how you ran Terraform previously (GitHub, env vars, no tfvars in git, Jenkins credential injection, Vault, Consul); the testing-to-prod lifecycle. Round 3 — Linux, networking, Docker (0:30-0:45): the df-shows-free-but-full triage (inodes, file descriptors, quotas, /tmp); open ports via ss/lsof; A-to-B port check via telnet/nc; the ping/traceroute/telnet connection triage; UDP vs TCP; image size optimization and the RUN/layer/whiteout questions. Round 4 — Kubernetes/AKS (0:45-1:05): pod troubleshooting (get/describe/logs/exec/events); restart loops and exit codes (137 = OOMKilled); the resource request/limit math (16Gi request vs 2Gi need); Pending pods and the node-provisioning checklist; taints vs tolerations vs labels; liveness vs readiness; the Python GIL readiness story; node pools vs one giant pool; App Gateway vs Load Balancer with the traffic flow."},
{"title": "The 90-Minute Mock Panel Script (Part 2: CI/CD, Security, Behavioral, Close)",
"body": "Round 5 — CI/CD, Jenkins, Helm, security (1:05-1:20): pipeline phases commit to release and where code is stopped most (unit tests); Groovy in Jenkinsfiles and shared libraries for standardization; why Helm over plain YAML; SAST/DAST/SCA gates and the Trivy image gate; secrets handling (Vault/Key Vault, never in git); RBAC least privilege across the stack. Round 6 — Architecture and scale (1:20-1:30): strangler fig on the monolithic trading platform; HA for mission-critical financial apps; observability at millions of txns/sec (sampling and aggregation); the multicloud strategy answer. Round 7 — Behavioral (1:30-1:45): the three STAR questions (unclear requirements, new technology, mentoring) plus 'prefer coding or infrastructure' — all timed at 2 minutes each, recorded. Round 8 — Your questions and close (1:45-1:50): ask two genuine questions ('How does the team handle on-call load?' 'What does the Terraform workflow look like here — modules, review, apply?'), then close: 'This is exactly the boundary work I do best — I would love to dig into your hardest reliability problem.' Scoring rubric per answer (1-5): accuracy (facts right), completeness (all parts of multi-part questions), structure (headline-first), delivery (pace, camera, no reading). Total 160; passing bar 120 with no reds on safety-critical items (secrets, RBAC, blast radius). Debrief within 24 hours: rewrite every yellow/red answer in full sentences."},
{"title": "The Cold-Answer Protocol: Training Without a Copilot",
"body": "The north star of this course is independent mastery: you answer with no copilot, no docs, no second chances — because the panel offers none. The protocol: (1) Daily timed drills — 8 items, 90 seconds each, aloud, scored red/yellow/green; (2) Weekly full mock — the 90-minute script, recorded; (3) Red-team review — for every red, write the full model answer by hand, then re-drill it cold the next day; handwriting recruits memory that typing does not; (4) Teach-back — explain each concept to a rubber duck or study partner as if they are the panel; if you cannot teach it simply, you do not know it; (5) The question bank never shrinks — every question in the spec list stays in rotation until it is green three sessions running. Worked example of a red-to-green cycle: Monday you blank on the for_each import escaping. Monday night you write terraform import 'azurerm_managed_disk.example[\"disk1\"]' <id> ten times with the quoting explained. Tuesday cold drill: green. The panel does not test what you can look up; it tests what you carry. Carry everything in this module, and the interview becomes a demonstration, not an examination."},
]

